<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karl Kautsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Karl Johann Kautsky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karl Kautsky was an Austrian-born Marxist theorist, for decades the leading theorist of the Social <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (SPD) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-international">Second International</a>, often called the Pope of Marxism. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, he founded the journal Die Neue Zeit in 1883 and edited it for 35 years, and wrote the theoretical section of the SPD's 1891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> Program. He defended orthodox Marxism against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein">Eduard Bernstein</a>'s revisionism and opposed the revolutionary spontaneity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a>. Opposing the war, he co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">Independent Social Democratic Party of Germany</a> in 1917, and he denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> as a premature coup. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> after the 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> and died there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky.jpg" alt="Karl Kautsky" width="330" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Kautsky_High_Res.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His party held that reform could only be a means to revolution, and <b>Karl Kautsky</b> attacked him, but Bernstein’s influence grew after the First World War.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
